; Mirror's Edge VR - optional settings
;
; Rename this file to  mevr.ini  and leave it beside d3d9.dll in the game's Binaries folder.
; That is the whole installation: d3d9.dll and mevr.ini in the same directory.
;
; It is also read from %LOCALAPPDATA%\MirrorsEdgeVR\ (beside mevr.log) if you would rather not
; put files in the game folder. Beside the DLL wins if both exist, and the log names the file
; it actually read.
;
; With no file present every setting keeps its compiled default.
;
; Almost everything here can also be set by a hotkey in game, and the hotkey wins - so a run
; without this file can reach the same states.
;
; D3D9Ex is the exception, and cannot be otherwise: a graphics device's type is fixed when it
; is created, so there is no moment later at which a key could change it.
;
; Every line is reported in the log at startup - applied, or rejected with the reason. A
; misspelt key is never silent:
;
;     [cfg] ...the full path of the file it read, so there is never any doubt which one...
;     [cfg]   FrameCap = 72
;     [cfg]   unknown key 'FrmeCap' - ignored
;     [cfg] 1 applied, 1 ignored. Hotkeys still override anything set here.
;
; Binaries is safe. TdGame\Config is NOT - Mirror's Edge hash-checks the files in there and
; refuses to start when they are edited, which cost two runs to learn. Nothing in this file
; belongs anywhere near it.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- debugging

; On by default. Turn it off for normal play and the mod becomes invisible: no text on the
; screen, and no hotkey does anything.
;
; Three keys stay live, because none of them is a diagnostic and each one leaves you stuck
; if it is missing:
;
;   PAGE UP      recentre the view. A headset put on at an angle needs this, and needs it
;                more with debugging off, since nothing else can correct the seating.
;   PAUSE (hold) close the game cleanly, so the engine writes its save.
;   F6           rescan for the view matrix. Stereo arms itself, but if it gives up it says
;                "press F6 by hand" - so the key has to still be there to press.
;
; Everything else - the overlay, the stereo strength and IPD controls, the animation locks,
; the frame cap, the capture and occlusion experiments - is off.
Debug = on


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- Phase 1 motion hands (experimental)
;
; The pose-input foundation is under active development. MotionHands currently samples the
; controller grip poses but does not move Faith's arms yet. Leave it OFF for ordinary play.
; MotionHandsDebug adds bounded pose validity/tracking transitions and a five-second report of
; grip/aim positions relative to the headset to mevr.log.

MotionHands = off
MotionHandsDebug = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- freeing the two grips
;
; On a Touch pair every button is already spoken for, and the two grips are the only controls
; that reach jump and quick turn. Anything that wants the grips for itself has to move those two
; actions first. These are the two settings that do it, kept separate because they answer
; different questions.
;
; StickJumpTurn puts jump on the right stick pushed UP and quick turn on the right stick pushed
; DOWN. It costs the pad's pitch axis, which is the one stick action a headset already does
; better - your head carries the pitch, and the game's own pitch is fought all frame by the view
; path anyway. Nothing else moves: turn stays on the right stick's X, and the grips keep working
; too, so with this on alone there are simply two ways to do each action.
;
; GripToGrip hands the grips to the hand instead: squeeze to close Faith's fingers, let go to
; open them. While it is on the grips no longer reach the game at all - a hand closing around
; nothing must not also jump - so it needs StickJumpTurn, and turns it on for you if you forgot.
; Turning StickJumpTurn on by itself is fine and changes nothing else.
;
; The hand closes proportionally, not on a threshold: half a squeeze is a half-closed hand. The
; fist itself was captured from Faith's own first-person run cycle, so it is the pose her
; animators authored for this mesh, thumb included, rather than something approximated here.
;
; Each hand is independent, and both stand down wherever the game poses the hands itself - a
; ledge, a pipe, a zipline, a melee - so a squeeze never fights an authored grab.

StickJumpTurn = off
GripToGrip = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- arm swing locomotion (measuring)
;
; Swing your arms to run, raise both hands to jump, crouch to crouch. None of that is built yet.
;
; What is here is rung AS.0, which MEASURES and moves nothing: it works out whether a real arm
; swing can be told apart from a head turn, a body turn, a gesture and a crouch, by logging four
; candidate ways of scoring the motion. The pad is not touched, so a run with this on plays
; exactly as it does with it off.
;
; ArmSwingDebug is the one that produces the data - a line a second, to mevr.log. Leave both off
; unless you are helping with the measurement.
;
; Hotkey: NUMPAD * toggles ArmSwing.

ArmSwing = off
ArmSwingDebug = off

; The two gestures, independently switchable because they fail differently.
;
; Jump needs BOTH hands clearly above your head - one hand never gets there during a swing, so
; the gesture cannot be produced by accident while running.
;
; Crouch measures your head against a standing height it works out for itself over the first
; couple of seconds. PAGE UP re-seeds it, so recentre while standing. Turn it off if you play
; seated: a seated baseline makes leaning forward look like a crouch.
;
; Turning Crouch off still leaves the swing suppressed while you squat - that is not this
; setting, it is the metric refusing to read a dropping head as arm motion.

ArmSwingJump = on
ArmSwingCrouch = on

; ---- Parkour: hands on the wall (PK.1) ----
;
; Hand-over-hand along a ledge, the way VR climbing games do it: squeeze a grip and that hand is
; anchored to the world, so moving it moves your BODY by the inverse. Pull your hands left and
; you travel right. Release one hand, move it, grab again, and the anchor switches to it.
;
; Because the game shimmies at one fixed speed rather than letting us place the body, this runs
; as a servo - it measures how far the body ACTUALLY moved and drives the stick until that
; matches how far you pulled. Pull faster than about 0.6 m/s and the body lags, then catches up.
;
; Lateral is measured against the player's own left/right, not a fixed room axis, so turning
; around in your room does not reverse the controls. Deliberately capped below full stick
; deflection: on a pipe, full deflection is a different move (the slide), and the ledge looks the
; same way. The physical stick keeps sole access to the extreme and overrides this when touched.
;
; Turning this on also stands the arm-swing gestures DOWN whenever the player is on a ledge,
; pipe or bar. That is not optional tidiness: the crouch gesture asserts the same input as
; letting go, and hands-overhead is the resting posture of someone hanging from a ledge.
Parkour = on

; A gripping hand within reach of the ledge is handed back to the GAME's own hang pose, which
; already places it correctly with the fingers over the edge. Releasing gives the hand back to
; the controller. Gripping in open air still closes a fist on nothing, which is why this is gated
; on proximity and not on the grip alone.
ParkourLedgeSnap = on

; Moving a GRIPPED hand up or down moves the view with it, 1:1 - the axes the body cannot travel
; on while hanging, so the camera answers them alone.
;
; The vertical accumulates motion rather than measuring a position, which is what makes a grab
; move the view by nothing: the frame the anchor hand changes has no previous sample to
; difference against, so it contributes an exact zero. The cost is that an integrator has no
; restoring force - push a hand down while gripped, release, raise it, grip again, and the offset
; is kept - so a long hang can walk the view above the body. Clamped, and reset on leaving.
ParkourCamLeadUp = on

; The same idea fore/aft - push the wall away and a hanging body swings back - but measured from
; the last grab rather than accumulated, so each grab settles the view back to a true depth.
;
; Not a style choice: integrated, this axis ratchets you INTO the ledge, because a shimmy pull
; brings the hand back toward the body every stroke and nothing gives it back. Fore/aft has a
; hard boundary a few centimetres away and the vertical does not, which is the whole reason the
; two axes are built differently.
ParkourCamLeadFwd = on

; PK.7. While direct drive is writing the pawn's position, hand the stick the direction the
; player is travelling instead of pinning it at zero.
;
; Not a movement input - the position writes win outright, measured at 0.00 UU/frame of fight
; over 26 reports. It is a QUESTION. The game answers it by advancing MoveLedgeLocation when
; there is more ledge, and by populating PendingShimmyCornerAnimation when there is a corner,
; and it does neither while the stick is silent. With the stick silent, every end-of-ledge and
; corner guard in the mod is inert: bFoundLedge never went false 903 UU out over open air, the
; corner field read None on all 194 samples of a run, and the stall detector keys off a stick
; this mode holds at zero.
;
; The blocker that stops you climbing past the end arms itself only after it has SEEN
; MoveLedgeLocation advance. If asking turns out not to populate it, nothing blocks and you get
; today's behaviour rather than a player frozen against a wall that is not there.
;
; CAPS LOCK toggles it live.
ParkourAskTheGame = on

; One-shot dump of every authored parkour-geometry class in the level, with instance counts and
; positions. Off, and it should stay off unless you are asking that question: it walks the whole
; object table twice and STALLS the game for about a second. Enabled, it waits for a quiet moment
; - in a level, off the wall, a minute in - because the first version fired on the first ledge
; grab and broke it.
;
; It has already answered its question. This level has one TdLedgeWalkVolume, 19000 UU away, and
; no authored class for grab-ledges at all.
ParkourGeomCensus = off

; The shimmy animation carries 55 degrees of camera pitch and 10 of roll on its own schedule.
; This forces LockAnimPitch/Roll/Yaw while on a wall and restores whatever you had set on the
; way out, so the view answers your hands rather than the animation.
ParkourLockAnim = on

; EXPERIMENTAL. Writes the pawn's position directly and stops driving the stick at all, so the
; game never plays a shimmy animation - movement, camera and arms all follow your hands.
;
; This writes a value the grab move also computes for itself, which is the pattern that has
; broken falls and landings elsewhere in this project. It stands down on any state change, on an
; oversized step, and when the game stops reporting a ledge under you, and it never writes the
; vertical axis - so the worst failure moves you along a wall rather than off it. SCROLL LOCK
; toggles it live.
ParkourDirectBody = on


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- frame pacing

; The engine's MaxSmoothedFrameRate. NUMPAD7 cycles 60 / 72 / 90 / 120 / 144 / 250 / 62.
;
; What matters is not the number but whether it divides the headset's refresh EXACTLY: a
; frame held for 2 display periods every time is smoother than a faster rate held for 2
; then 3. At 120 Hz that means 60; at 144 Hz it means 72. Press NUMPAD7 in game and the log
; reports the ratio for the headset actually attached.
;
; 62 is the game's own shipped default, kept for comparison. 250 is effectively uncapped.
FrameCap = 60


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- animation locks
;
; Mirror's Edge moves the camera by itself constantly - wall-run roll, landing dips, vaults.
; These cancel that motion per axis, leaving the view where your head put it.
;
; All OFF by default, which means the animation plays as the game shipped it. Turning one ON
; locks that axis against the animation.
;
; Yaw is the one to leave off in most cases: an animation that turns the player is carrying
; them somewhere, and cancelling it leaves the body facing one way and the view another.
;
; Hotkeys: NUMPAD2 pitch, NUMPAD4 roll, NUMPAD5 yaw.

LockAnimPitch = off
LockAnimRoll  = off
LockAnimYaw   = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- graphics path

; How the frame reaches the headset. Both of these exist for one reason: if the fast path
; misbehaves on your machine, you need a way back that does not require a different build.
;
; On by default, and worth leaving on - together they are what took the frame grab from about
; 4 ms to 0.4 and the frame rate from 75-80 to over 100.

; Ask Windows for a D3D9Ex device instead of the plain one the game requests. Everything below
; depends on it: a shared surface is an Ex feature, so D3D9Ex = off also forces FastCapture off.
;
; No hotkey, and there cannot be one - see the note at the top. If the game will not start or
; renders nothing at all, this is the first thing to turn off.
;
; An empty file named  mevr_noex.txt  beside d3d9.dll does the same thing and beats this
; setting. It exists for the case where the game fails before it gets far enough to be worth
; editing anything, and because an empty file can be created from a file manager without
; opening a text editor.
D3D9Ex = on

; Copy the frame on the GPU rather than dragging it through system memory. About ten times
; faster, and the only thing that could go wrong with it is tearing - two devices share one
; surface, and if the ordering between them ever fails you would see a torn strip rather than
; a wrong image.
;
; Hotkey: NUMPAD8, so you can compare both in one session.
FastCapture = on

; Render at a size the game's own video menu does not offer.
;
; Without this the frame is 2560x1440, an eye gets half of it - 1280x1440 - and the runtime
; upscales that to the 2112x2304 it asked for. More than half the pixels the headset shows are
; invented. The menu is not the real limit though: the engine reads ResX/ResY from
; TdEngine.ini and keeps them, but VALIDATES them against the display modes the graphics
; driver reports and silently snaps to the nearest when yours is not one. This adds yours to
; that list, so the check passes. Nothing ever changes your monitor's mode - the mod already
; forces the game windowed, so the oversized frame is just a surface that Present shrinks into
; the window. The headset is fed from that surface BEFORE the shrink, at full size.
;
; Resolution = auto follows whatever headset is attached and needs nothing else set. It also
; keeps ResX / ResY in TdEngine.ini in step for you, which is the half people miss.
;
; ⚠️ auto always lands ONE LAUNCH LATE, by construction. The engine fixes its render size
; from its config long before it loads d3d9.dll, so the only code of ours that runs early
; enough is DllMain - and the headset cannot be asked there, because opening the OpenXR
; runtime loads a DLL and doing that under the loader lock is a deadlock. So the size is
; recorded when the headset IS reachable (first frame) and applied at the next launch. Change
; headsets, or move Virtual Desktop's render-scale slider, and the log says:
;
;     *** [res] auto: the runtime now wants 2064x2208 an eye, this run was built for
;         2112x2304. RESTART to pick it up
;
; Restart once and it is right. The remembered size lives in
; %LOCALAPPDATA%\MirrorsEdgeVR\headset.txt - delete it to force a fresh look.
;
; TO PIN A SIZE INSTEAD, both of these, and one alone does nothing:
;
;   1. Resolution = <W>x<H> here.
;   2. The SAME numbers as ResX / ResY in the [SystemSettings] section of
;      Documents\EA Games\Mirror's Edge\TdGame\Config\TdEngine.ini
;
; What to put there. Read "[xr] headset wants WxH per eye" out of the log, DOUBLE the width,
; and then take 9/16 of that width for the height - not the height the log prints. Mirror's
; Edge renders 16:9 whatever you hand it and letterboxes the rest into a separate, smaller
; buffer, which the mod can only answer by dropping to mono. So 2112x2304 per eye means
; 4224x2376 here: full width per eye, 3% more vertical than the runtime asked for, and an
; aspect the engine does not fight. auto does exactly this arithmetic.
;
; ⚠️ This is the single most expensive setting in this file. 4224x2376 is 2.7x the pixels of
; 1440p, and MaxMultisamples=4 in TdEngine.ini multiplies that again - turn MSAA down to 1
; first, the mod downsamples into the eye buffers anyway. Measured 2026-08-29: the engine
; allocates the whole scene target set at 4224x2376 with room to spare (the exe is large
; address aware, 3.2 GB free at first Present) and held 72 fps - but that was a MONO
; measurement, with duplication not yet armed. Stereo issues every scene draw twice. If the
; frame rate falls far enough the engine starts moving work into its 1280x720 buffers, which
; is the streaking failure the half-res section below is about - so come down a step
; (3840x2160, 3200x1800) rather than living with it.
;
; Off = the game's own menu list is the limit, and the engine snaps anything else. Turning it
; off also repairs TdEngine.ini on its own: the engine finds the size is not a real display
; mode any more, snaps to the nearest and writes that back. MaxMultisamples is NOT restored -
; that one is yours.
Resolution = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- the half-res hunt (diagnostics)
;
; Some runs come up soft: the engine quietly renders part or all of the scene into its
; 1280x720 buffer set instead of the full-resolution one, and the picture looks like 720p
; upscaled. Every log now carries a [mode] line per 12-second window - "healthy" or
; "DEGRADED" - plus [rt-first]/[surf]/[blit] lines that show which buffers the engine chose.
; The three test keys below are levers for reproducing it on the monitor, with no headset -
; they default off and none belongs in normal play. PinMinDesiredFps is different: it is the
; PREVENTION, and it defaults ON.
;
; TestStall injects ten 400 ms frames at the given frame number (0 = off) - an artificial
; load hitch. TestWideFov performs the same culling-FOV widening a headset run performs.
; OcclusionMode is the DELETE hotkey's setting made persistent: 0 auto, 1 every occlusion
; query reports visible, 2 never override.
;
; PinMinDesiredFps holds the engine's Client.MinDesiredFrameRate at 1 - the threshold its
; degrade-when-slow logic compares frame times against (the ini ships it at 35). Every
; half-res entry on record lands on a genuine work spike (stereo engagement at the end of a
; level load), and the monitor-side "slow frame" exonerations both used sleeps, which a
; scaler measuring work rather than wall time never sees. Pinned to 1, "too slow" cannot
; happen at any playable frame rate, so the engine should never move scene work into the
; half-size buffers at all. Turn it off only to hunt the trigger itself.
;
; The visible symptom - hands and building edges streaking along their motion history - is
; duplication running while the engine is in the half-res state, and it is guarded
; automatically: the mod drops to a clean mono view within a couple of seconds of detecting
; it, stands its own load down (duplication, the occlusion override and the widened FOV all
; pause), and keeps retrying - stereo resumes by itself the moment the engine renders clean
; again, with a backoff ladder so a stubborn run settles into flat instead of flapping.
; Stereo also arms 120 frames later than it used to: every measured onset sat right on the
; end of a level load, so the arm now waits out the engine's most fragile moment. No
; setting: the guard has no off switch because a run with it off has nothing to recommend
; it.

TestStall = 0
TestWideFov = off
OcclusionMode = 0
PinMinDesiredFps = on


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- beam balance
;
; On a narrow beam the game tips you and expects a counter on the strafe stick. This lets you
; lean your head instead, which is quicker to start than moving a thumb - and the game gives you
; only 0.8 s to answer before you fall.
;
; Head roll is the one axis of head tracking the game never sees, so this collides with nothing.
; The stick keeps working either way.
;
; If leaning tips you the WRONG way, flip BEAM SIGN in the F3 overlay (NUMPAD . to select,
; NUMPAD + or - to change). The handedness is measured rather than assumed.

BalanceHeadRoll = on
